Before the 205, Peugeot was considered the most conservative of France's "big three" car manufacturers, producing large [[sedan (car)|saloons]] such as the [[Peugeot 504|504]] and [[Peugeot 505|505]], although it had entered the modern supermini market in 1973 with the [[Peugeot 104]]. The genesis of the 205 lay within Peugeot's takeover in 1978 of [[Chrysler]]'s [[Chrysler Europe|European]] divisions [[Simca]] and the former [[Rootes Group]], which had the necessary expertise in making small cars including the [[Simca 1100]] in France and [[Hillman Imp]] in Britain. It was around this time that Peugeot began to work on the development of a new supermini for the 1980s.

After its main launch in February 1983, the right-hand drive form for the UK market followed in September that year.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.classicandperformancecar.com/features/octane_features/245797/driven_peugeot_205_gti_and_t16.html |title=Driven: Peugeot 205 GTI and T16 | work = Octane |publisher=Classic and Performance Car |date=December 2009 | first = John | last = Simister |archive-date=21 August 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140821220118/http://www.classicandperformancecar.com/features/octane_features/245797/driven_peugeot_205_gti_and_t16.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> Shortly after its launch, it was pipped to the [[European Car of the Year]] award by the similar sized [[Fiat Uno]], but ultimately (according to the award organizers) it would enjoy a longer high market demand than its Italian competitor.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.caroftheyear.org/previous-winners/1984_1/coty |title=Previous winners |publisher=Car of the year |access-date=4 December 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120616045053/http://www.caroftheyear.org/previous-winners/1984_1/coty |archive-date=16 June 2012 }}</ref> It was one of five important small cars to be launched onto the European market within a year of each other: the other four were the Uno, the second generation [[Ford Fiesta]], the original [[Opel Corsa]] (sold as the [[Vauxhall Nova]] on the British market) and the original [[Nissan Micra]]. Its launch also closely followed that of the [[Austin Metro]] and [[Volkswagen Polo]] Mk2.

The styling of the 205 is often thought to be a Pininfarina design, although [[Gerard Welter]] claims that it is an in-house design; [[Pininfarina]] only styled the Cabriolet.<ref>{{cite news | url = https://www.telegraph.co.uk/motoring/4755873/Keeping-track-I-knew-that.-Or-did-I.html | archiveurl = https://web.archive.org/web/20160303021338/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/motoring/4755873/Keeping-track-I-knew-that.-Or-did-I.html | archivedate = 2016-03-03 | title = Keeping track: I knew that. Or did I | newspaper = [[The Daily Telegraph|The Telegraph]] | first = Peter | last = Dron | date = 2002-04-13 }}</ref> It is often credited as the car that turned Peugeot's fortunes around.

The fully independent suspension used the standard [[PSA Peugeot Citroën]] layout that had debuted in the [[Peugeot 305]] estate. A key ingredient of the success of the 205, it had [[MacPherson strut]]s at the front and trailing arms with [[torsion bar suspension|torsion bars]] at the rear. The rear suspension was very compact, designed to minimise suspension intrusion into the boot, giving a wide flat loadspace, while providing excellent ride and handling.

Early 205s used the [[PSA X engine|X]] [[petrol engine|petrol]] engine{{refn|group=n|commonly nicknamed the Douvrin "Suitcase Engine"}} from the older [[Peugeot 104]], although these were later (1987–1988) replaced with the newer [[PSA XU engine|XU]] and [[PSA TU engine|TU]]-series engines, which were of PSA design. [[Engine]]s ranged in [[engine displacement|displacement]] from 954&nbsp;cc to 1905&nbsp;cc, in [[carburettor]] or [[fuel injection|fuel injected]] versions.

The [[Diesel engine|diesel]] models employed the [[PSA XUD]] engine, lifted from the [[Citroën BX]] which was introduced in September 1982. These engines had a capacity of 1769&nbsp;cc (XUD7) and 1905&nbsp;cc (XUD9) and are closely related to the XU5 and XU9 petrol engines in the BX16 and BX19 of the time. The diesel engines were world-beating and so petrol-like that many buyers were won over by petrol car performance combined with diesel economy. For instance, the 205 GRD (1.8 Diesel, {{convert|59|bhp|kW|0|abbr=on}} at 4,600 rpm, {{convert|110|Nm|lbft Nm|0|abbr=on|order=out}} at 2,000 rpm) was as fast as, yet smoother than, the 205 GR (1.4 Petrol, {{convert|59|bhp|kW|0|abbr=on}} at 5,000 rpm, {{convert|106.9|Nm|lbft Nm|0|abbr=on|order=out}} at 2,500 rpm),<ref>{{citation |title=Alle Auto's 1987 |last=Dubbelman |first=Huub |p=137/219 |publisher=Uitgeverij De Alk BV |location=Alkmaar |language=nl |series=Grote alken |volume=824 |year=1986 |isbn=90-6013-824-4 }}</ref> due to the engine developing its higher peak torque at lower rpm, while using much less fuel.

